WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Forward propagation.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.852
This is the network actually computing.

3
00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:07.867
Do it by hand once, and every diagram gets simple.

4
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:14.067
In a forward pass, data enters the network and each layer does the same three things.

5
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.633
A matrix multiply, a bias add, and an activation.

6
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:20.500
Then it hands the result to the next layer.

7
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.933
Inference is a single forward pass.

8
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.233
Let's trace a small network by hand.

9
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:29.426
Two inputs, two hidden units, and one output.

10
00:00:29.426 --> 00:00:31.033
Data flows left to right.

11
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:33.267
Here's layer one.

12
00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:37.100
The weight matrix is two by two, and the input is one and zero.

13
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.667
The first row times the input gives zero point five.

14
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:42.400
The second row gives one.

15
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.767
Now follow the numbers all the way through.

16
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.633
The input is one and zero.

17
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:51.000
The first linear step gives zero point five and one.

18
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.700
Tanh bends them to zero point four six and zero point seven six.

19
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.900
The second linear step gives zero point one two.

20
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.567
And sigmoid turns that into a probability of about zero point five three.

21
00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.133
Here it is in NumPy.

22
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:09.967
The at sign does the matrix multiply and we add the bias.

23
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.900
Then tanh bends the result.

24
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.367
One more linear step and a sigmoid.

25
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.967
The printout matches our hand calculation.

26
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.067
Here's why the hand trace pays off.

27
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:22.691
Your model crashes with a shape error.

28
00:01:22.691 --> 00:01:26.667
Thirty two by two hundred one, against two hundred by sixty four.

29
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:30.638
Because you've traced shapes by hand, you read it instantly.

30
00:01:30.638 --> 00:01:35.533
The data has two hundred one flux bins, but the first layer expects two hundred.

31
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:37.952
Someone changed the binning upstream.

32
00:01:37.952 --> 00:01:40.300
You fix the preprocessing, not the model.

33
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.313
Let's recap.

34
00:01:42.313 --> 00:01:45.700
Each layer multiplies, adds a bias, and activates.

35
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.900
Inference is one forward pass.

36
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.567
Shapes must chain, and shape errors are the most common bug.

37
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:54.187
So trace one pass by hand, once.

38
00:01:54.187 --> 00:01:56.000
Next up: backpropagation.
